TRANSITION PHASE: HERO -> ABOUT (The Heat Bleed)

Vibe: Descending into the volatile, high-energy core of the system.
Goal: Seamlessly bridge the highly structured Hero UI into the interactive
plasma physics of the About section without breaking the "Future" aesthetic.

SCROLL MECHANICS & PARALLAX

Trigger: Standard native scroll. The transition begins when the user scrolls
past 10vh (meaning the Hero section starts moving up).

Hero Exit (Parallax): The elements in the Hero section (Avatar, TOTO TOFU text)
do not scroll at a 1:1 ratio with the page. They scroll slightly slower
(e.g., transform: translateY(Y * 0.4)), creating a feeling of depth as the
About section slides up over them.

Background Continuity: The deep matte slate (#0F172A) background remains
continuous. There is no hard border or color block separating the two sections.

VISUAL EFFECTS (The "Thermal Bleed")

As the boundary between Hero and About crosses the bottom of the viewport,
the "Fire/Heat" element is visually introduced.

The Melting Prompt:

The "Scroll to descend ↓" bouncing text at the bottom of the Hero section
is the first casualty of the heat.

As it approaches the top third of the viewport (during scroll), it applies
an SVG displacement map (or CSS filter: url(#heat-ripple)), making it
literally "melt" and distort into a heat haze before fading to opacity: 0.

The Ambient Glow:

A massive, highly diffused radial gradient (e.g., radial-gradient(circle at  center top, rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.05) 0%, transparent 70%)) bleeds upwards
from the top boundary of the About section into the Hero section, hinting
at the digital plasma below.

CURSOR STATE HANDOFF

The most critical part of this transition is the seamless handoff of the
mouse tracking logic.

Threshold: When the About section occupies > 50% of the viewport (tracked
via Intersection Observer).

State Change:

The clip-path Gacha Mask from the Hero section is immediately unmounted
or disabled to free up CPU/GPU resources.

The cursor style shifts from the default crosshair to the "Plasma Shooter"
reticle (a minimal SVG circle with four tick marks).

The event listener tracking the mouse switches its output: instead of driving
a clipping mask, it now drives the "Cooling/Magnetic Stabilizer" effect
for the heat-haze text in the About section.

TECHNICAL UX NOTE

Do not use heavy WebGL for the transition boundary itself. Stick to CSS/SVG
filters (like <feTurbulence> and <feDisplacementMap>) applied conditionally
based on scroll position. This ensures the 60fps scrolling performance remains
silky smooth as the user enters the heavy physics zone of the About section.